{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 15, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful ruler who reigned over the County of Tripoli from 1146 to 1193. He was a significant figure in the Crusades and a prominent leader of the Frankish states in the Levant. His reign saw considerable expansion of Tripoli's territory and influence a period of relative stability and prosperity for the county.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond III was the son of Raymond I Count of Tripoli. He inherited the county upon his father's death in 1146. He proved to be a capable and ambitious ruler quickly consolidating his power and expanding Tripoli's territory through military campaigns and strategic alliances. He faced challenges from rival counts and other powers vying for control of the region. His early reign was marked by a focus on strengthening Tripoli's defenses and securing its borders against Muslim incursions. He also fostered trade and economic growth benefiting from the lucrative trade routes connecting Europe and the East.\n\nRaymond III's reign was characterized by significant military achievements. He engaged in numerous conflicts against the Seljuk Turks and other Muslim forces. He successfully defended Tripoli from several sieges and expanded its territory into Syria and Lebanon. He was a skilled military commander known for his strategic acumen and tactical brilliance. His military campaigns secured Tripoli's position as a major power in the Levant and solidified its role as a vital link in the Crusader network. He also engaged in diplomatic efforts to maintain peace and stability in the region.\n\n*Political and Military Campaigns* Raymond III's reign witnessed a flourishing of Tripoli's political and military power. He implemented reforms to strengthen the county's administration and improve its defenses. He fostered alliances with other Crusader states and European powers to counter Muslim aggressi